course: dictionary
lesson: 8543
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. «Scaffold» can mean a framework of poles and planks around a building, a raised platform in history, or support given to learners. The context tells you which meaning the writer means.
explain: «Michelangelo painted the Sistine Chapel ceiling standing on a scaffold and bending his head back, not lying down.» Painting a ceiling and standing up with your head bent back are clues. The painter needed something high to stand on, so scaffold means the building framework.
explain: Always ask: what word would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence. Could a painter stand on a platform where criminals were executed? No, so that meaning does not fit this sentence.
explain: In school, teachers scaffold tasks by giving hints, word banks and examples. Once you can do the task alone, the support is taken away.
fact: A scaffold is a framework of metal poles and planks that workers stand on while building or repairing.
fact: The whole structure is usually called scaffolding.
fact: «Scaffold» can also mean a raised wooden platform where criminals were executed in public, as with Anne Boleyn and Charles I.
fact: To scaffold learning means to give support step by step, then take it away as the learner gets better.
fact: Scaffold is also a verb: teachers scaffold tasks with hints, word banks and examples.
exercise: «Michelangelo painted the Sistine Chapel ceiling standing on a scaffold.» What is a scaffold here? | A raised wooden platform for executions | A tall painted ceiling | A type of paint | *A framework of poles and planks that workers stand on
exercise: «He wrote a funny poem about his aching neck.» Which word shows the painting was uncomfortable? | poem | funny | *aching | wrote
exercise: In school, what does it mean to scaffold learning? | To test learners on a raised platform | To build a wall around the classroom | *To give support step by step, then take it away as the learner improves | To give everyone the same hard task
test: Keep away from the scaffolding; it is dangerous to climb. What does scaffolding mean here? | A school test | A kind of ladder for climbing trees | *The whole framework of poles and planks around a building | A type of paint for ceilings
test: «Anne Boleyn died on the scaffold.» What does scaffold mean in this history sentence? | *A raised wooden platform where criminals were executed in public | A hint or word bank in class | A tall painted ceiling | A framework of metal poles for builders
exam: «Michelangelo painted the Sistine Chapel ceiling standing on a scaffold and bending his head back.» What does scaffold most likely mean here? | A kind of paint | *A framework of poles and planks that a worker stands on | A platform where criminals were executed | A school hint or word bank
